<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Papua New Guinea</h1><p class="st-meta">1975 CE – present · capital Port Moresby</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Eastern half of New Guinea and offshore islands, independent from Australia in 1975, with a Westminster system and about 840 languages.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/territory-of-papua-and-new-guinea">Territory of Papua and New Guinea</a> was administered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-somare">Michael Somare</a> was elected chief minister in 1972, the Whitlam government in Australia instituted self-governance in late 1973, and independence followed in 1975 as a Commonwealth realm. On 1 September 1975 Bougainville declared independence, a dispute resolved by an August 1976 agreement.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Governments have been coalitions since 1972, and no-confidence votes ousted Somare in 1980 and 1985. Tribal fighting increased in the highlands in the 1970s and gradually became gang violence. On Bougainville, an uprising began in 1988 and became a civil war after the mine closed in 1989; a ceasefire was reached in January 1998 and the Bougainville Peace Agreement was signed in 2001. Elections use the Limited Preferential Vote. About 97% of the land is customary land held by communities.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>Bougainville voted for independence by 98.31% in a non-binding referendum on 23 November 2019. Peter O'Neill resigned as prime minister in May 2019 and James Marape replaced him, continuing in office after the 2022 election. In March 2025 the constitution was amended to adopt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> as the official state religion. Charles III is king, represented by a governor-general.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/papua-new-guinea.jpg" alt="Papua New Guinea" width="330" height="248"><figcaption>Image: User:Nightstallion · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Papua_New_Guinea.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1975 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Port Moresby</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Michael Somare, Julius Chan, Peter O'Neill, James Marape</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity in several denominations, often combined with traditional beliefs</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English, Tok Pisin, Hiri Motu</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/papua-new-guinea">Papua New Guinea</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Independent State of Papua New Guinea</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=2000&amp;focus=papua-new-guinea" aria-label="Papua New Guinea on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8741 2531 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Papua New Guinea lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M9061 2787L9061 2787"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M9061 2787h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M9061 2787h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M9061 2787h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Port Moresby. <a href="/atlas?year=2000&amp;focus=papua-new-guinea">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
